---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Heart rate zones split your effort into five bands, from an easy warm-up to an all-out sprint,
      each defined as a range of beats per minute. They are built from your maximum heart rate, which
      this calculator estimates from your age using either the classic 220 minus age or the more
      accurate Tanaka formula. Add your resting heart rate and it switches to the Karvonen method,
      which personalises the zones to your fitness. Zone 2, around 60 to 70 percent of your maximum, is
      the easy aerobic pace most endurance training should live in.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Heart Rate Zone Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="the-zones">
    <h2>What the Zones Mean <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Each zone trains something different. Zone 1, 50 to 60 percent, is a gentle warm-up or recovery.
      Zone 2, 60 to 70 percent, is easy conversational effort that builds your aerobic base and is where
      the bulk of endurance work belongs. Zone 3, 70 to 80 percent, is a moderate, slightly harder
      steady effort. Zone 4, 80 to 90 percent, is hard work near your lactate threshold that raises your
      ceiling. Zone 5, 90 to 100 percent, is maximal effort you can only hold for short bursts, used
      sparingly in intervals.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="max-heart-rate">
    <h2>Estimating Maximum Heart Rate <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Every zone is a fraction of your maximum heart rate, so the estimate of that maximum matters. The
      familiar 220 minus age is easy to remember but rough, with real values scattered around ten beats
      either way. The Tanaka formula, 208 minus 0.7 times your age, tracks the population a little better,
      particularly for older adults. Neither is a substitute for a measured maximum from a hard supervised
      test or a chest-strap monitor, but for planning everyday training they are close enough.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="karvonen">
    <h2>Personalising With the Karvonen Method <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A flat percentage of your maximum ignores how fit you are. The Karvonen method fixes that by working
      from your heart-rate reserve, the difference between your resting and maximum heart rates. It takes a
      percentage of that reserve and adds your resting rate back on. Because a fitter person has a lower
      resting heart rate and therefore a bigger reserve, their zones shift to match. Add your resting heart
      rate here and the zones recalculate to reflect your individual fitness.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Most training is easy">
      <p>A common mistake is spending too much time in the moderate middle. Well-structured endurance plans keep most sessions easy in Zone 2 and reserve hard efforts for a small share of the week.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Trusting the formula as exact">
      <p>
        A formula maximum can be off by ten beats or more. If your monitor regularly shows readings above
        the estimate during hard efforts, trust the monitor and adjust your zones accordingly.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Making easy days too hard">
      <p>
        Zone 2 should feel genuinely easy, a pace you could hold a conversation at. Creeping into Zone 3
        on every run blunts recovery and stalls progress. Slow down to speed up.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Add your resting rate">
      <p>Entering a morning resting heart rate switches the tool to the Karvonen method for zones tailored to your fitness. Everything runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
